<div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
  <div class="mb-8">
    <a href="/docs" class="inline-flex items-center text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 font-medium transition-colors group">
      <svg class="w-4 h-4 mr-2 transform group-hover:-translate-x-1 transition-transform" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"></path></svg>
      Back to Documentation
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-12 gap-8 lg:gap-12">
    <!-- Sidebar Partial -->

    <!-- Main Content -->
    <main class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-12 space-y-12">
      <!-- Header Section -->
      <div class="relative">
        <div class="absolute -inset-1 bg-gradient-to-r from-emerald-500 to-teal-600 rounded-lg blur opacity-25"></div>
        <div class="relative">
          <h1 class="text-5xl font-extrabold text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-emerald-400 via-teal-400 to-cyan-400 mb-4 tracking-tight">Columnar Storage</h1>
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed">
             SoliDB includes a specialized columnar storage engine optimized for analytics, reporting, and read-heavy workloads. It features column-oriented storage, aggressive compression, and fast aggregations.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Overview Section -->
      <section id="overview" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-800/20 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:border-white/10 transition-colors">
          <div class="flex items-center mb-6">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-emerald-500 text-white font-bold text-lg mr-4 shadow-[0_0_15px_rgba(16,185,129,0.5)]">
              <i class="fas fa-chart-bar text-sm"></i>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Overview</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
            Unlike traditional row-oriented collections where data is stored document-by-document, columnar collections store data <strong>column-by-column</strong>. This architecture is vastly superior for analytical queries where you typically access only a few columns but scan millions of rows.
          </p>
          <div class="mb-8 rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-white/10 shadow-2xl">
            <img src="/images/docs/columnar-vs-row.png" alt="Columnar vs Row-Oriented Storage" class="w-full h-auto">
          </div>

          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4">
             <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl p-4 border border-white/5">
              <div class="text-emerald-400 font-bold mb-1 flex items-center"><i class="fas fa-compress-alt mr-2"></i> Compression</div>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400">LZ4 compression often achieves 2-4x better storage efficiency than row storage.</p>
            </div>
             <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl p-4 border border-white/5">
              <div class="text-teal-400 font-bold mb-1 flex items-center"><i class="fas fa-filter mr-2"></i> Pruning</div>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400">Read only the columns you need. IO is reduced proportionally to the number of columns skipped.</p>
            </div>
             <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl p-4 border border-white/5">
              <div class="text-cyan-400 font-bold mb-1 flex items-center"><i class="fas fa-layer-group mr-2"></i> Grouping</div>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400">Efficient <code>GROUP BY</code> and aggregation operations (SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX).</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl p-4 border border-white/5">
              <div class="text-blue-400 font-bold mb-1 flex items-center"><i class="fas fa-code mr-2"></i> Typed</div>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400">Explicit schemas with supported types: Int64, Float64, String, Bool, Timestamp, Json.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Columnar vs Time Series Explanation -->
          <div class="mt-8 bg-indigo-500/10 border border-indigo-500/20 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-indigo-300 mb-4 flex items-center">
              <i class="fas fa-balance-scale mr-2"></i> Columnar vs. Standard Time Series
            </h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-300 mb-4">
              SoliDB offers two distinct approaches for handling time-based data. Choosing the right one depends on your ingestion and query patterns.
            </p>
            
            <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-white/10">
              <table class="min-w-full text-left text-sm">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-400">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="p-3 font-semibold">Feature</th>
                    <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-indigo-300">Standard Time Series</th>
                    <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-emerald-300">Columnar Storage</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-300">
                  <tr>
                    <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-400">Storage Layout</td>
                    <td class="p-3">Row-oriented (Log Structured)</td>
                    <td class="p-3">Column-oriented (Chunked)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-400">Best For</td>
                    <td class="p-3">High-throughput ingestion, real-time monitoring, simple retrieval</td>
                    <td class="p-3">Complex analytics, reporting, aggregations over large history</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-400">Ingestion</td>
                    <td class="p-3">Fast individual inserts &amp; appends</td>
                    <td class="p-3">Favors batched bulk inserts</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-400">Retention</td>
                    <td class="p-3">TTL &amp; Pruning supported</td>
                    <td class="p-3">Manual management</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-400">Primary Ops</td>
                    <td class="p-3"><code>INSERT</code>, <code>GET range</code></td>
                    <td class="p-3"><code>GROUP BY</code>, <code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code></td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            
            <div class="mt-4 p-3 bg-blue-900/20 rounded border border-blue-500/20 text-xs text-blue-200 flex items-start">
               <i class="fas fa-lightbulb mt-0.5 mr-2"></i>
               <span>
                 <strong>Recommendation:</strong> Use <strong>Time Series collections</strong> for raw data ingestion and real-time dashboards. Offload historical data to <strong>Columnar collections</strong> for long-term storage and heavy analytical reporting.
               </span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Creating Collections Section -->
      <section id="creating" class="scroll-mt-24">
         <div class="bg-gray-800/20 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:border-white/10 transition-colors">
          <div class="flex items-center mb-6">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-indigo-500 text-white font-bold text-lg mr-4 shadow-[0_0_15px_rgba(99,102,241,0.5)]">
              <i class="fas fa-plus text-sm"></i>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Creating Collections</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6">
            Columnar collections require an explicit schema definition at creation time. This allows the engine to optimize storage layout.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
             <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
               <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-green-500/20 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold rounded">POST</span>
               <code class="text-gray-300">/_api/database/:db/columnar</code>
             </div>

            <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{
  "name": "server_metrics",
  "compression": "lz4",
  "columns": [
    { "name": "timestamp", "type": "TIMESTAMP" },
    { "name": "host", "type": "STRING" },
    { "name": "cpu_usage", "type": "FLOAT64" },
    { "name": "requests", "type": "INT64" },
    { "name": "metadata", "type": "JSON", "nullable": true }
  ]
}</code></pre>
          </div>
          
           <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
             <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-white/5 rounded text-xs text-gray-400 border border-white/10">INT64</span>
             <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-white/5 rounded text-xs text-gray-400 border border-white/10">FLOAT64</span>
             <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-white/5 rounded text-xs text-gray-400 border border-white/10">STRING</span>
             <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-white/5 rounded text-xs text-gray-400 border border-white/10">BOOL</span>
             <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-white/5 rounded text-xs text-gray-400 border border-white/10">TIMESTAMP</span>
             <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-white/5 rounded text-xs text-gray-400 border border-white/10">JSON</span>
           </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Inserting Data Section -->
      <section id="inserting" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-800/20 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:border-white/10 transition-colors">
          <div class="flex items-center mb-6">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-blue-500 text-white font-bold text-lg mr-4 shadow-[0_0_15px_rgba(59,130,246,0.5)]">
              <i class="fas fa-file-import text-sm"></i>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Inserting Data</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6">
            Insertions should ideally be batched for maximum performance. The engine automatically handles decomposition into columns and compression.
          </p>

          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6">
            Every row is checked against the columns before anything is written. A value of the wrong type, a missing or <code>null</code> value in a column that is not <code>nullable</code>, a column the collection does not have, or a row that is not an object refuses the whole insert with <code>400</code> and names the row: <code>row 1: column 'cpu_usage' is float64, got a string</code>. An integer in a <code>float64</code> column is stored as a float. A <code>timestamp</code> takes a string or an integer; a <code>json</code> column takes anything. Columns are not nullable unless their definition says <code>"nullable": true</code>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
             <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
               <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-green-500/20 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold rounded">POST</span>
               <code class="text-gray-300">/_api/database/:db/columnar/:collection/insert</code>
             </div>

            <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{
  "rows": [
    {
      "timestamp": "2024-01-01T10:00:00Z",
      "host": "us-east-1a",
      "cpu_usage": 45.2,
      "requests": 1024
    },
    {
      "timestamp": "2024-01-01T10:00:01Z",
      "host": "us-east-1a",
      "cpu_usage": 48.1,
      "requests": 1150
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>
          </div>

          <p class="text-gray-300 mt-6 mb-4">
            A query can insert too, which is how a columnar collection is filled from documents. Every row of the query goes in as one insert; it takes no <code>OPTIONS</code>, binds no <code>NEW</code>, and is refused inside a transaction. <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>UPSERT</code> and <code>REMOVE</code> do not apply to a columnar collection.
          </p>
          <pre><code class="language-sdbql text-sm">FOR e IN events
  FILTER e.status == "done"
  INSERT { host: e.host, cpu_usage: e.cpu, requests: e.count } INTO metrics</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Deleting Section -->
      <section id="deleting" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-800/20 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:border-white/10 transition-colors">
          <div class="flex items-center mb-6">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-red-500 text-white font-bold text-lg mr-4 shadow-[0_0_15px_rgba(239,68,68,0.5)]">
              <i class="fas fa-eraser text-sm"></i>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Deleting Rows</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6">
            A row is deleted by the id its insert returned, several by their ids, or all those a filter keeps. Rows are not updated in place: delete and insert again. A block that loses rows is written again once per call, so delete many rows in one request, not one request per row. A row policy (<code>ROW_POLICY("metrics", "doc.tenant == CURRENT_USER")</code>) applies to every query that reads the collection, for everyone but an admin; such a query builds the rows for the policy to see, so the column arrays are not used and it is slower. <code>/query</code> and <code>/aggregate</code> cannot apply a policy and answer <code>403</code> to a caller it binds. An insert, a delete and a truncate each send one change event, whatever the number of rows: <code>{"type": "insert", "key": "&lt;first id&gt;", "data": {"rows": 5000, "first": "&lt;id&gt;", "last": "&lt;id&gt;"}}</code>. A changefeed subscribed to the collection receives it, and a live query that reads the collection runs again. The event is sent on the node that took the write, and it does not carry the rows. No trigger fires on a columnar collection. A foreign key links documents: it cannot be declared on a columnar collection nor towards one. Declaring a trigger or a foreign key there is refused, with that reason.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
                <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-red-500/20 text-red-400 text-xs font-bold rounded">DELETE</span>
                <code class="text-gray-300">/_api/database/:db/columnar/:collection/row/:id</code>
              </div>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">One row. <code>404</code> when the collection has no row of that id.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
                <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-green-500/20 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold rounded">POST</span>
                <code class="text-gray-300">/_api/database/:db/columnar/:collection/delete</code>
              </div>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mb-4">Either <code>ids</code> or a <code>filter</code>, never both. A filter is a comparison (<code>EQ</code>, <code>NE</code>, <code>GT</code>, <code>GTE</code>, <code>LT</code>, <code>LTE</code>, <code>IN</code>) or an <code>and</code> / <code>or</code> of filters. The response counts the rows deleted; ids that are not there are passed over.</p>
              <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{
  "filter": {
    "and": [
      { "column": "host", "op": "EQ", "value": "us-east-1a" },
      { "column": "requests", "op": "LT", "value": 100 }
    ]
  }
}</code></pre>
              <pre class="mt-4"><code class="language-json text-sm">{ "status": "ok", "deleted": 12 }</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
                <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-yellow-500/20 text-yellow-400 text-xs font-bold rounded">PUT</span>
                <code class="text-gray-300">/_api/database/:db/columnar/:collection/truncate</code>
              </div>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Deletes every row and keeps the columns. Requires the Admin permission on the database, like truncating a document collection.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="columns" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-800/20 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:border-white/10 transition-colors">
          <div class="flex items-center mb-6">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-sky-500 text-white font-bold text-lg mr-4 shadow-[0_0_15px_rgba(14,165,233,0.5)]">
              <i class="fas fa-table-columns text-sm"></i>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Changing the Columns</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6">
            A column can be added, renamed or dropped while the collection holds rows. All three need Admin.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
                <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-green-500/20 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold rounded">POST</span>
                <code class="text-gray-300">/_api/database/:db/columnar/:collection/columns</code>
              </div>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mb-4">Adds a column. Only the schema is written: the rows already stored read <code>null</code> in it, which is why a column added this way is always nullable. Rows inserted afterwards may hold it.</p>
              <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{ "name": "region", "type": "string" }</code></pre>
              <pre class="mt-4"><code class="language-json text-sm">{ "status": "added", "column": "region" }</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
                <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-yellow-500/20 text-yellow-400 text-xs font-bold rounded">PUT</span>
                <code class="text-gray-300">/_api/database/:db/columnar/:collection/columns/:column</code>
              </div>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mb-4">Renames a column. Its values are kept; queries use the new name from then on and a row that names the old one is refused.</p>
              <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{ "name": "value" }</code></pre>
              <pre class="mt-4"><code class="language-json text-sm">{ "status": "renamed", "column": "value", "was": "v" }</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
                <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-red-500/20 text-red-400 text-xs font-bold rounded">DELETE</span>
                <code class="text-gray-300">/_api/database/:db/columnar/:collection/columns/:column</code>
              </div>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mb-4">Drops a column and its values, for good: a column added later under the same name starts empty. The last column cannot be dropped.</p>
              <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{ "status": "dropped", "column": "junk" }</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>

          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mt-6 space-y-2 text-gray-400 text-sm">
            <li>A rename and a drop write every block again, without its rows being read one by one: on 200,000 rows of three columns each took about 40&nbsp;ms. An add writes nothing but the schema.</li>
            <li>A column with an index is neither renamed nor dropped: drop its index first.</li>
            <li>The change is replicated, and a node that joins receives the collection with its columns as they are.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="retention" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-800/20 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:border-white/10 transition-colors">
          <div class="flex items-center mb-6">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-amber-500 text-white font-bold text-lg mr-4 shadow-[0_0_15px_rgba(245,158,11,0.5)]">
              <i class="fas fa-hourglass-half text-sm"></i>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Retention</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6">
            A collection can keep its rows for a set time. A row's age is counted from the instant it was inserted, which is in its id; the rows older than the retention are removed by a background pass, once a minute. Rows are stored in the order they were inserted, so a block whose rows are all too old goes as one key, with nothing read or written again; at most one block, the one the cutoff falls in, is written again with the rows it keeps.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
                <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-yellow-500/20 text-yellow-400 text-xs font-bold rounded">PUT</span>
                <code class="text-gray-300">/_api/database/:db/columnar/:collection/retention</code>
              </div>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mb-4">Needs Admin: it removes rows for good. <code>seconds</code> is one or more; <code>null</code> keeps every row again. Nothing is removed by the call itself. <code>GET &hellip;/columnar/:collection</code> shows the setting as <code>retention_secs</code>.</p>
              <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{ "seconds": 2592000 }</code></pre>
              <pre class="mt-4"><code class="language-json text-sm">{ "name": "metrics", "retention_secs": 2592000 }</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
                <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-green-500/20 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold rounded">POST</span>
                <code class="text-gray-300">/_api/database/:db/columnar/:collection/prune</code>
              </div>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mb-4">Removes now what the retention no longer keeps, instead of waiting for the next pass. With no retention it removes nothing.</p>
              <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{ "status": "ok", "deleted": 6000 }</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>

          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mt-6 space-y-2 text-gray-400 text-sm">
            <li>In a cluster the retention is replicated and each node removes its own old rows; the removals are not.</li>
            <li>An index on a column goes on answering after the oldest rows are removed (from 2.12.1; before, it answered for none of the rows that stayed until it was created again).</li>
            <li>A row replicated or restored under an older id is as old as that id says.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Aggregations Section -->
      <section id="compacting" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-800/20 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:border-white/10 transition-colors">
          <div class="flex items-center mb-6">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-emerald-500 text-white font-bold text-lg mr-4 shadow-[0_0_15px_rgba(16,185,129,0.5)]">
              <i class="fas fa-compress text-sm"></i>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Compacting</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6">
            Rows are stored in blocks of up to 65,536. An insert of 4,096 rows or more is sealed as a block of its own, so a loader that sends 5,000 rows at a time leaves thirteen blocks where one would do, and deleting rows leaves blocks that hold what they kept. Compacting merges the runs of neighbouring blocks that fit together in one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
              <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-green-500/20 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold rounded">POST</span>
              <code class="text-gray-300">/_api/database/:db/columnar/:collection/compact</code>
            </div>
            <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mb-4">The rows, their order and what every query answers stay what they were; the indexes are not touched. On a million rows loaded 5,000 at a time, 200 blocks became 16 in a second.</p>
            <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{ "status": "ok", "blocks_before": 200, "blocks_after": 16 }</code></pre>
          </div>

          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mt-6 space-y-2 text-gray-400 text-sm">
            <li>It runs when asked, not on its own. Inserts wait while one run of blocks is merged, and go on between two runs.</li>
            <li>Each node holds its rows in blocks of its own: compacting is not replicated.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="aggregations" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-800/20 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:border-white/10 transition-colors">
          <div class="flex items-center mb-6">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-purple-500 text-white font-bold text-lg mr-4 shadow-[0_0_15px_rgba(168,85,247,0.5)]">
              <i class="fas fa-calculator text-sm"></i>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Aggregations</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6">
            Perform fast aggregations on single columns or with grouping. The engine reads only the necessary columns and uses streaming processing for minimal memory footprint (O(1)).
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-6">
            <code class="text-cyan-400">POST &hellip;/columnar/:collection/aggregate</code> takes a <code>filter</code>, the one <code>/query</code> takes (a comparison, or <code>{"and": [&hellip;]}</code> / <code>{"or": [&hellip;]}</code>): only the rows it keeps are aggregated, found the way <code>/query</code> finds them. <code>{"column": "value", "operation": "AVG", "group_by": ["metric_id"], "filter": {"column": "day", "op": "GTE", "value": 1764547200000}}</code> is the average per metric since that day: 32&nbsp;ms for 500 groups over the last 30 days of 3.65 million rows. Through 2.12.0 the endpoint ignored <code>filter</code> and answered for every row.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-8">
            <!-- Simple Aggregate -->
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
               <h4 class="text-purple-400 font-bold mb-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-wider flex items-center">
                 <i class="fas fa-calculator mr-2"></i> Simple Aggregate
               </h4>
               <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-4">Calculate metrics across the entire collection.</p>
               
               <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                 <div>
                   <div class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase mb-2">SDBQL</div>
                   <pre><code class="language-sql text-sm">FOR c IN cpu_logs
  COLLECT AGGREGATE
    avg_usage = AVG(c.usage)
  RETURN { avg_usage }</code></pre>
                 </div>
                 <div>
                   <div class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase mb-2">API</div>
                   <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{
  "column": "usage",
  "operation": "AVG"
}</code></pre>
                 </div>
               </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Group By -->
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
               <h4 class="text-purple-400 font-bold mb-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-wider flex items-center">
                 <i class="fas fa-layer-group mr-2"></i> Group By
               </h4>
               <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-4">Group results by one or more columns.</p>

               <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                 <div>
                   <div class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase mb-2">SDBQL</div>
                   <pre><code class="language-sql text-sm">FOR r IN requests
  COLLECT
    host = r.host
  AGGREGATE
    total = SUM(r.count)
  RETURN { host, total }</code></pre>
                 </div>
                 <div>
                   <div class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase mb-2">API</div>
                   <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{
  "column": "count",
  "operation": "SUM",
  "group_by": ["host"]
}</code></pre>
                 </div>
               </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Time Bucket -->
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
               <h4 class="text-purple-400 font-bold mb-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-wider flex items-center">
                 <i class="fas fa-clock mr-2"></i> Time Bucket
               </h4>
               <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-4">Group by fixed time intervals (e.g., 1h, 5m). Optimized for time-series.</p>

               <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                 <div>
                   <div class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase mb-2">SDBQL</div>
                   <pre><code class="language-sql text-sm">FOR m IN metrics
  COLLECT
    time = TIME_BUCKET(m.ts, '1h'),
    host = m.host
  AGGREGATE
    avg_cpu = AVG(m.cpu)
  RETURN { time, host, avg_cpu }</code></pre>
                 </div>
                 <div>
                   <div class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase mb-2">API</div>
                   <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{
  "column": "cpu",
  "operation": "AVG",
  "group_by": ["TIME_BUCKET(ts, '1h')", "host"]
}
// Note: SDBQL is recommended for this</code></pre>
                 </div>
               </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="mt-6">
            <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-3">Supported Operations</h4>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
             <span class="px-3 py-1 bg-purple-500/20 text-purple-300 rounded-full text-xs font-mono">SUM</span>
             <span class="px-3 py-1 bg-purple-500/20 text-purple-300 rounded-full text-xs font-mono">AVG</span>
             <span class="px-3 py-1 bg-purple-500/20 text-purple-300 rounded-full text-xs font-mono">COUNT</span>
             <span class="px-3 py-1 bg-purple-500/20 text-purple-300 rounded-full text-xs font-mono">MIN</span>
             <span class="px-3 py-1 bg-purple-500/20 text-purple-300 rounded-full text-xs font-mono">MAX</span>
             <span class="px-3 py-1 bg-purple-500/20 text-purple-300 rounded-full text-xs font-mono">COUNT_DISTINCT</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-3">This endpoint (and the drivers' <code>aggregate</code>) takes these six, one per call. Standard deviation, variance, median, percentiles and an approximate distinct count are aggregates of a query, which also takes several at once and any filter: <code>FOR r IN metrics COLLECT h = r.host AGGREGATE p = PERCENTILE(r.ms, 95), s = STDDEV(r.ms) RETURN {h, p, s}</code>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Filtering Section -->
      <section id="filtering" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-800/20 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:border-white/10 transition-colors">
          <div class="flex items-center mb-6">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-pink-500 text-white font-bold text-lg mr-4 shadow-[0_0_15px_rgba(236,72,153,0.5)]">
              <i class="fas fa-filter text-sm"></i>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Filtering & Querying</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-6"><code class="text-cyan-400">POST …/columnar/:collection/query</code> and the driver's <code class="text-cyan-400">query_columnar</code> take the same request: <code>columns</code> (all of them when left out), a <code>filter</code> (<code>{"column","op","value"}</code>, or <code>{"and":[…]}</code> / <code>{"or":[…]}</code>), <code>order_by</code> (<code>"column [ASC|DESC]"</code>, on a returned column), <code>offset</code> and <code>limit</code>. The driver's <code class="text-cyan-400">aggregate_columnar</code> takes the same <code>filter</code>.</p>

          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6">
            Columnar collections are a first-class SDBQL data source: <code class="text-cyan-400">FOR</code> reads
            them like any other collection, so <code class="text-cyan-400">FILTER</code>,
            <code class="text-cyan-400">SORT</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">LIMIT</code>, joins and subqueries
            all work, and you can combine columnar data with documents, edges and vector search in a single query.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6 mb-6">
             <div class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase mb-3">SDBQL</div>
            <pre><code class="language-sql text-sm">FOR m IN cpu_logs
  FILTER m.host == "us-east-1a"
  SORT m.timestamp DESC
  LIMIT 100
  RETURN { ts: m.timestamp, cpu: m.cpu_usage }</code></pre>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6 mb-6">
             <div class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase mb-3">Joining columnar data with documents</div>
            <pre><code class="language-sql text-sm">FOR m IN cpu_logs
  FOR h IN hosts
    FILTER h._key == m.host
    RETURN { cpu: m.cpu_usage, region: h.region }</code></pre>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-amber-900/10 border border-amber-500/20 rounded-xl p-4 flex items-start mb-6">
            <i class="fas fa-gauge-high text-amber-400 mt-1 mr-3"></i>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">Performance note.</strong> A
              <code>FOR … FILTER … COLLECT … AGGREGATE</code> is computed over the column arrays, without building a row,
              when its FILTERs compare columns with constants (<code>==</code> with a string, a number or a boolean;
              <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code> with a number, joined by
              <code>AND</code>), its keys are columns and its aggregates are <code>COUNT</code>, <code>SUM</code>,
              <code>AVG</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code>, <code>COUNT_DISTINCT</code>,
              <code>STDDEV</code> / <code>VARIANCE</code>, <code>MEDIAN</code>, <code>PERCENTILE(col, p)</code> or
              <code>APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT</code>, <code>BIT_AND</code> / <code>BIT_OR</code> /
              <code>BIT_XOR</code> of a column (the sums in milliseconds for millions of rows, a
              median or a percentile a few times that, an exact distinct count the slowest). <code>UNIQUE</code>,
              <code>SORTED_UNIQUE</code>, <code>COLLECT_LIST</code> and <code>COLLECT &hellip; INTO</code> return the
              values themselves, in the order of the rows: they build each row, as does a query that reads the
              collection under a row policy or inside a window function. One window shape is read from the arrays:
              the first rows of each partition, <code>LET n = ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY r.a ORDER BY r.b DESC)
              FILTER n &lt;= 3</code> right after the <code>FOR</code>. Any other query over a columnar
              collection (a <code>RETURN</code> of rows, <code>!=</code>, <code>OR</code>, a function in a key) builds
              each row first, then filters: correct, and slower on a large collection.</p>
          </div>

          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
            The REST endpoint remains available and pushes the filter into the storage layer, applying it
            <em>before</em> projection to minimize IO:
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
             <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
               <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-green-500/20 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold rounded">POST</span>
               <code class="text-gray-300">/_api/database/:db/columnar/:collection/query</code>
             </div>

            <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{
  "columns": ["timestamp", "cpu_usage"],
  "filter": {
    "column": "host",
    "op": "EQ",
    "value": "us-east-1a"
  },
  "limit": 100
}</code></pre>
          </div>

           <div class="mt-6">
            <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-3">Filter Operators</h4>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
             <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-pink-500/20 text-pink-300 rounded text-xs font-mono">EQ (=)</span>
             <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-pink-500/20 text-pink-300 rounded text-xs font-mono">NE (!=)</span>
             <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-pink-500/20 text-pink-300 rounded text-xs font-mono">GT (>)</span>
             <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-pink-500/20 text-pink-300 rounded text-xs font-mono">GTE (>=)</span>
             <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-pink-500/20 text-pink-300 rounded text-xs font-mono">LT (<)</span>
             <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-pink-500/20 text-pink-300 rounded text-xs font-mono">LTE (<=)</span>
             <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-pink-500/20 text-pink-300 rounded text-xs font-mono">IN</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-4">
              A <code>timestamp</code> column is compared by instant, whatever the writing of the stored value or of the filter's:
              ISO&nbsp;8601 text (<code>2026-01-01T00:00:00Z</code>, <code>2026-01-01 01:00:00+01:00</code>, <code>2026-01-01</code>; no offset means UTC)
              or a number of milliseconds since the epoch, read as seconds below 10,000,000,000 as the SDBQL date functions do.
              <code>{"column": "timestamp", "op": "GTE", "value": "2026-01-01"}</code> keeps the rows from that day on, and an <code>EQ</code> finds an instant
              inserted with another offset or as a number. A range against a value that is not a date answers <code>400</code>.
              Rows whose value is null or not a date are kept by no range. Indexes do not serve a <code>timestamp</code> column.
              This is the REST and driver filter: an SDBQL <code>FILTER</code> compares the stored values as they are, strings as strings.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Indexing Section -->
      <section id="indexing" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-800/20 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:border-white/10 transition-colors">
          <div class="flex items-center mb-6">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-yellow-500 text-white font-bold text-lg mr-4 shadow-[0_0_15px_rgba(234,179,8,0.5)]">
              <i class="fas fa-bolt text-sm"></i>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Advanced Indexing</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6">
            SoliDB supports advanced columnar index types to accelerate specific query patterns. Indexes are created independently of the data and can be added or removed at any time.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-blue-500/10 border border-blue-500/20 rounded-xl p-5 mb-6">
            <h4 class="text-blue-300 font-semibold mb-2 text-sm">What needs no index</h4>
            <ul class="text-sm text-gray-300 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside">
              <li>A filter is compared in the arrays of the blocks, in SDBQL (<code>FOR r IN metrics FILTER &hellip;</code>) and in <code>/query</code> alike. Each block of an integer or float column knows its smallest and largest value, and a block they rule out is passed over: a range of timestamps or of sequential ids reads the blocks that hold it. On a million rows, a hundred rows of a range come back in under a millisecond with no index.</li>
              <li>A range against a number (<code>&lt;</code> <code>&lt;=</code> <code>&gt;</code> <code>&gt;=</code>) is always answered that way, with no index: a <code>sorted</code> index is not consulted for it. The <code>minmax</code> index type that existed for ranges through 2.11 was removed: creating one answers <code>400</code>, and one a collection still holds is dropped the first time the collection is loaded.</li>
              <li>An equality or an <code>IN</code> uses a <code>hash</code>, <code>sorted</code>, <code>bitmap</code> or <code>bloom</code> index when the column has one, for the rows the index holds; rows inserted after the index was built are compared in the arrays. Removing the oldest rows (a retention, a delete of everything before a date) leaves the index in use; deleting rows from the middle leaves it answering for the rows before them. <code>GET &hellip;/indexes</code> says how many rows each index holds (<code>rows</code>), and <code>POST &hellip;/index?rebuild=true</code> builds one again over every row.</li>
              <li>An index is a modest gain here: the arrays are compared fast. On 3.65 million rows, the 7,300 rows of one value of an integer column come back in 6.6&nbsp;ms with a sorted index and 8.3&nbsp;ms without; an index on a column whose values follow the insertion order (a day, a sequence) brings nothing, the blocks' bounds already skip what is out of range.</li>
              <li>A count whose <code>FILTER</code> compares columns with constants (<code>FOR r IN metrics FILTER r.t &gt;= @from AND r.t &lt; @to COLLECT WITH COUNT INTO n RETURN n</code>, or <code>LENGTH(FOR &hellip; RETURN 1)</code>) is counted in the arrays and builds no row: half a million rows of a range are counted in about a millisecond. <code>EXPLAIN</code> reports it as <code>columnar_count</code>.</li>
              <li><code>EXPLAIN</code> reports <code>columnar_scan</code>, the index an equality used (<code>"index_used": "host (hash)"</code>), and a note: <em>`metrics` is columnar: 3 of its 6 columns are built, for the rows its FILTER on `host`, `v` keeps, found in the column arrays and the index on host (hash)</em>.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
               <h4 class="text-yellow-400 font-bold mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-wider flex items-center">
                 <i class="fas fa-layer-group mr-2"></i> Bitmap Index
               </h4>
               <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">
                 Best for <strong>low-cardinality</strong> columns (e.g., status, category, boolean flags). Uses highly compressed bitsets (LZ4) to perform blazing fast equality and <code>IN</code> lookups.
               </p>
               <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs text-gray-500 font-mono bg-black/30 p-2 rounded">
                 <span>Type: <code>"bitmap"</code></span>
                 <span class="w-1 h-1 bg-gray-600 rounded-full"></span>
                 <span>Ideal for: &lt; 100 distinct values</span>
               </div>
            </div>

            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
               <h4 class="text-yellow-400 font-bold mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-wider flex items-center">
                 <i class="fas fa-hashtag mr-2"></i> Hash Index
               </h4>
               <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">
                 Best for <strong>exact match</strong> queries (equality) on high-cardinality columns (e.g., UUIDs, user IDs). O(1) lookups but does not support range queries.
               </p>
               <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs text-gray-500 font-mono bg-black/30 p-2 rounded">
                 <span>Type: <code>"hash"</code></span>
                 <span class="w-1 h-1 bg-gray-600 rounded-full"></span>
                 <span>Fastest for: Equality</span>
               </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
               <h4 class="text-yellow-400 font-bold mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-wider flex items-center">
                 <i class="fas fa-filter mr-2"></i> Bloom Filter Index
               </h4>
               <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">
                 Best for <strong>probabilistic membership</strong> testing. Checks if a value <em>might</em> exist in a chunk before scanning it. Useful for skipping chunks for equality checks on unique or semi-unique columns.
               </p>
               <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs text-gray-500 font-mono bg-black/30 p-2 rounded">
                 <span>Type: <code>"bloom"</code></span>
                 <span class="w-1 h-1 bg-gray-600 rounded-full"></span>
                 <span>No False Negatives</span>
               </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
               <h4 class="text-yellow-400 font-bold mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-wider flex items-center">
                 <i class="fas fa-list mr-2"></i> Sorted Index (Default)
               </h4>
               <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">
                 Standard index storing, for each value, the list of its rows. Used for equality and <code>IN</code>. A range against a number is answered from the arrays instead, which is faster: the index holds one entry per distinct value and a range had to read each of them.
               </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-8 bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
             <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
               <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-green-500/20 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold rounded">POST</span>
               <code class="text-gray-300">/_api/database/:db/columnar/:collection/index</code>
             </div>

            <pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{
  "column": "status",
  "index_type": "bitmap"
}</code></pre>

            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-4">
              The answer comes once the index is built: <code>{"status": "created", "column": "status", "index_type": "bitmap"}</code>. Two sorted indexes on 3.65 million rows take 1.2 seconds; inserts are not held up meanwhile.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-6 bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
            <h4 class="text-white font-bold mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-wider">Building in the background</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-4">
              With <code>?wait=false</code> the request answers at once, <code>202</code>, with the id of the build, and the index is built on a thread of the server. <code>GET &hellip;/index/:column</code> follows it.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">curl -X POST "$URL/_api/database/app/columnar/metrics/index?wait=false" \
  -d '{"column": "metric_id", "index_type": "sorted"}'
# 202
# {"status": "building", "job": "0193a1…", "column": "metric_id", "index_type": "sorted",
#  "rows_done": 0, "rows_total": 3650000, "started_at": 1794300000}

curl "$URL/_api/database/app/columnar/metrics/index/metric_id"
# {"status": "building", "job": "0193a1…", "rows_done": 1310720, "rows_total": 3650000, …}
# then
# {"status": "created", "job": "0193a1…", "column": "metric_id", "index_type": "sorted",
#  "created_at": 1794300001, "rows": 3650000, "collection_rows": 3650000, "missing": 0,
#  "size_bytes": 3912345}</code></pre>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mt-4 space-y-2 text-gray-400 text-sm">
              <li>An index holds the rows that were there when it was built, and nothing rebuilds it: <code>rows</code> is how many it answers for, <code>collection_rows</code> how many the collection holds, <code>missing</code> the difference. The rows it lacks are still found, compared in their blocks; rebuild (<code>?rebuild=true</code>) when <code>missing</code> has grown.</li>
              <li><code>status</code> is <code>building</code>, <code>created</code>, or <code>failed</code> with an <code>error</code>. A column with neither an index nor a build answers <code>404</code>.</li>
              <li>A build is written down before it starts: if the server stops before it ends, it is started again when the server next reads the collection, under the same <code>job</code>.</li>
              <li><code>DELETE &hellip;/index/:column</code> calls off a build that is still running.</li>
              <li><code>GET &hellip;/indexes</code> lists, for each index, the rows it holds and its size, and under <code>builds</code> the builds that have not ended in an index.</li>
              <li><code>?rebuild=true</code>, with or without <code>wait=false</code>, builds the index again from the rows as they are now, in place of the one the column has.</li>
              <li>In a cluster the index is announced to the other nodes when the build is accepted; each builds its own.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

      <!-- Use Cases Section -->
      <section id="use-cases" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
          <!-- IoT / Sensor Data -->
          <div class="bg-gray-800/20 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:border-white/10 transition-colors">
            <div class="flex items-center mb-4">
              <div class="p-3 bg-orange-500/10 rounded-lg text-orange-400 mr-4">
                <i class="fas fa-microchip"></i>
              </div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">IoT & Sensor Data</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mb-4">
              Store high-frequency sensor readings from thousands of devices. Use Time Bucket aggregations to downsample data for visualization (e.g., convert 1-second resolution to 5-minute averages).
            </p>
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded p-4 border border-white/5">
              <div class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase mb-2">SDBQL</div>
              <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs">FOR s IN sensors
  FILTER s.region == 'us-east-1'
  COLLECT
    bucket = TIME_BUCKET(s.ts, '5m'),
    device = s.device_id
  AGGREGATE
    avg_temp = AVG(s.temperature),
    max_temp = MAX(s.temperature),
    avg_humidity = AVG(s.humidity)
  RETURN { 
    time: bucket, 
    device, 
    temp: avg_temp, 
    max: max_temp, 
    humidity: avg_humidity 
  }</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Financial / Trading -->
          <div class="bg-gray-800/20 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:border-white/10 transition-colors">
            <div class="flex items-center mb-4">
              <div class="p-3 bg-green-500/10 rounded-lg text-green-400 mr-4">
                <i class="fas fa-chart-line"></i>
              </div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">Financial Analytics</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mb-4">
              Analyze trade data to produce OHLCV (Open, High, Low, Close, Volume) candles and VWAP (Volume Weighted Average Price) efficiently.
            </p>
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded p-4 border border-white/5">
              <div class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase mb-2">SDBQL</div>
              <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs">FOR t IN trades
  COLLECT
    bucket = TIME_BUCKET(t.ts, '1m'),
    symbol = t.symbol
  AGGREGATE
    open = FIRST(t.price),
    high = MAX(t.price),
    low = MIN(t.price),
    close = LAST(t.price),
    volume = SUM(t.size),
    vwap_num = SUM(t.price * t.size)
  RETURN { 
    time: bucket, 
    symbol, 
    open, high, low, close, volume,
    vwap: vwap_num / volume 
  }</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

    </main>
  </div>
</div>
